<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Martin de Porres</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1579">1579</a>–<a href="/years/1639">1639</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Saint Martin de Porres</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Martin de Porres was a lay brother of the Dominican order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, the son of a Spanish nobleman and a freed Black woman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>. Known for his care for the sick and the poor, he became one of the most popular saints of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> and was canonised in 1962 as the first Black saint of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. He is the patron saint of people of mixed race and of social justice.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-de-porres.jpg" alt="Martin de Porres" width="260" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_Martin_de_Porres_huaycan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Martín de Porras Velázquez was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>, on 9 December 1579. He was the illegitimate son of a Spanish nobleman, Don Juan de Porras y de la Peña, and Ana Velázquez, a freed slave of African and Native descent. After the birth of his sister Juana in 1581 the father abandoned the family, and Ana supported her children by taking in laundry.</p><p>Martin was sent to a primary school for two years and then apprenticed to a barber surgeon. Under Peruvian law, descendants of Africans and Native Americans could not become full members of religious orders, so at 15 he asked the Dominicans of the Convent of the Rosary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> to accept him as a donado, a volunteer who did menial tasks in return for the habit and a place in the community.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Martin kept up his trades of barbering and healing and took on kitchen work, laundry and cleaning. After eight years the prior, Juan de Lorenzana, chose to overlook the law and let him take vows in the Third Order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-dominic">Saint Dominic</a>. Not all of the 300 men at the priory accepted this: one novice called him a mulatto dog, and a priest mocked his illegitimacy and descent from slaves.</p><p>In 1603, at 24, he professed vows as a Dominican lay brother; he never became a priest. At 34 he was put in charge of the infirmary, where he served until his death.</p><p>He cared for the sick inside and outside the convent, without distinction between Spanish nobles and slaves lately brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. When an epidemic struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, 60 friars were ill, and he is said to have passed through locked doors to reach them. He begged for alms that, in normal times, fed 160 poor people a day, and founded a residence for orphans and abandoned children. He did not eat meat. Many miracles were attributed to him, among them cures, levitation, bilocation and a rapport with animals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Martin died on 3 November 1639, aged 59, and was a friend of Saint Juan Macías and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rose-of-lima">Saint Rose of Lima</a>. As his body lay on display, each visitor snipped a piece of his habit as a relic. Pope Gregory XVI beatified him on 29 October 1837, and Pope John XXIII canonised him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 6 May 1962. His feast day is 3 November.</p><p>He is the patron saint of people of mixed race, barbers, innkeepers and public health workers, and is often shown as a young friar with a broom, since he held all work sacred, sometimes with a dog, a cat and a mouse eating from one dish. Schools, churches and a programme at the Las Casas Institute at Blackfriars Hall, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Martin de Porres” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Catholic Church organised parishes, missions and confraternities, and Lima produced Rose of Lima, canonised in 1671 as the first saint of the Americas, and <b>Martin de Porres</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In Lima <b>Martin de Porres</b>, son of a Spanish nobleman and a freed woman, was first admitted to the Dominicans only as a servant.</blockquote><a href="/societies/spanish-america">Social Hierarchy of Spanish America</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-12.06,-77.04&amp;year=1618" aria-label="Lima on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Martin de Porres"><image href="/maps/globes/g27000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6075 6024h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6075 6024h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-12.06,-77.04&amp;year=1618">What was here in 1618? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
